Present without waiting for the swap on the Switch, and say where the GL
thread's time goes.

- SMS_GX_ASYNC_PRESENT (on by default on the Switch, off elsewhere): the
  game's thread draws the XFB, the overlay and the menus with its GL calls
  queued, queues the swap for the GL thread and goes on to the next frame.
  It waited for the swap before, so every frame the game's own work, the
  driver's work on the GL thread and the GPU each waited for the others; now
  the next frame's game work runs while the GL thread and the GPU finish the
  last one. One frame is in flight: a present first waits for the last
  frame's swap (counted as the swap's time, so GXPC_SkipNextPresent still
  sees a slow present) (gx_platform.cpp)
- the overlay panels' GL work (which reads and restores GL state) is queued
  as one command for the GL thread, instead of a wait for it per state read
  (gx_render.cpp drawOverlayPanel)
- the GL thread's busy time and, with the swap queued, its time in
  SDL_GL_SwapWindow: GXPCTimes glBusy and glSwap, a line in the overlay
  (gx_glthread.cpp, gx_overlay.cpp)
- SMS_TIMING_LOG=1 logs the overlay's frame breakdown every 2 s, overlay
  shown or not, as the Android branch does
- on the Switch the GL thread starts on a core of its own and the texture
  pack's idle-priority work runs below the game's priority, through the
  runtime's sms_switch_gl_thread and sms_switch_background_thread (weak:
  gx_selftest.nro has neither)

